/// ReasoningEngineQuestionBank — 44 questions (38 single-select, 6 multi-select)
enum ReasoningEngineQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A client asks why their agent sometimes behaves with strict predictability and other times with creative flexibility in the same conversation. Which explanation is accurate?", options: [("A", "The reasoning runtime is a hybrid: a deterministic resolver enforces authored logic first, then an LLM reasoning loop makes probabilistic decisions inside those constraints."), ("B", "The agent randomly toggles between two entirely separate LLMs — one tuned for strict compliance and one tuned for open-ended conversation."), ("C", "Predictability only occurs in voice channel deployments; text channels are always fully probabilistic with no deterministic layer at all."), ("D", "The agent's temperature setting is adjusted mid-conversation by the end user's tone, producing the appearance of shifting predictability.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The runtime combines a deterministic resolver with an LLM reasoning loop — authored logic constrains what the LLM can decide, producing this exact mix of predictability and flexibility.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: There is no dual-LLM toggle mechanism; a single reasoning loop operates within resolver-enforced constraints.  \n* C: The deterministic/probabilistic split applies to all channels, not exclusively voice.  \n* D: There is no end-user-tone-driven temperature adjustment described in the runtime's architecture."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "An architect wants to explain the boundary between authored logic and LLM judgment to a new team member. Which statement is correct?", options: [("A", "The LLM decides which variables get set, while authored logic only decides which subagent responds to the user."), ("B", "Deterministic logic controls what the agent knows; the LLM controls whether and how to act on that knowledge."), ("C", "Authored logic and LLM judgment are interchangeable, since either layer can evaluate conditional branches equally well."), ("D", "The LLM evaluates all conditional branching directly, while authored logic only stores static, unevaluated text.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: This is the fundamental split — deterministic logic shapes what information and options exist; the LLM decides how to use them.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Variable-setting via `set` statements is deterministic and authored, not LLM-decided.  \n* C: Only the deterministic resolver evaluates conditions — the LLM never evaluates them directly.  \n* D: Authored logic actively evaluates conditionals; it is not merely static, unevaluated text."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "Why does the runtime compile the `.agent` file into an Agent Graph rather than interpreting it line-by-line at runtime?", options: [("A", "Interpreting line-by-line would require re-authenticating the running user on every single reasoning iteration, which compilation avoids entirely."), ("B", "Compilation reduces the number of LLM tokens consumed per turn, directly lowering the org's monthly credit bill."), ("C", "Compiling into a DAG for tool dependencies and an FSM for subagent transitions lets the runtime guarantee ordering and manage retries reliably."), ("D", "Line-by-line interpretation is technically impossible for AgentScript files exceeding roughly 500 lines of authored logic.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The compiled Agent Graph encodes a DAG for dependency ordering and an FSM for transition/retry logic — this is what gives the runtime predictable sequencing and retry behavior.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Re-authentication per iteration is not a described concern that compilation addresses.  \n* B: Token consumption and credit billing are unrelated to why the file is compiled into a graph.  \n* D: There's no described line-count limit that makes interpretation \"impossible\"; compilation is an architectural choice, not a workaround for a size limit."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "What does a \"backward arrow\" represent in the compiled Agent Graph's state machine?", options: [("A", "A visual indicator showing that a subagent has exceeded its allotted credit budget for the current session."), ("B", "A manual rollback action available to end users who want to undo their most recent request in the conversation."), ("C", "A deprecated transition path that the compiler automatically strips out during the publish step for performance."), ("D", "A retry edge recorded when the LLM attempts a tool call, receives an error, and reasons into an alternative approach.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Backward arrows are the FSM's mechanism for recording tool-call failures and the LLM's subsequent pivot to an alternative approach, preventing catastrophic workflow failure.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Credit budgets are a separate concern from FSM retry tracking.  \n* B: There's no end-user-facing rollback control described as a backward arrow.  \n* C: Backward arrows are retry records, not deprecated paths stripped during publish."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "During a single reasoning iteration, which of the following happen in Phase 1, before the LLM is ever invoked? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Evaluating `if`/`else if`/`else` conditions against current variable values."), ("B", "Filtering the tool schema using `available when` guards on each action."), ("C", "The LLM extracting a slot-fill parameter value from the conversation using `...`."), ("D", "Executing `run @actions.X` calls synchronously and capturing their outputs."), ("E", "The LLM producing a terminal text response that ends the reasoning loop.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Condition evaluation, `available when` filtering, and synchronous `run` execution are all deterministic Phase 1 steps that complete before the LLM is called.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Slot-fill extraction is a Phase 2 LLM action, not part of deterministic resolution.  \n* E: Producing a terminal response is the LLM's Phase 2 output, not a Phase 1 step."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "What exactly does the LLM receive when Phase 2 begins?", options: [("A", "One system prompt, full conversation history, the resolved instruction text matching current state, and schemas for actions that passed their guard."), ("B", "Two competing system prompts — the global one and any subagent override — merged so the LLM can weigh both."), ("C", "Only the current message with no conversation history, keeping every reasoning iteration fully independent and stateless."), ("D", "The complete raw `.agent` file, including all `if`/`else` blocks, so it can infer the author's intended logic paths.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Phase 2 input is precisely the resolved prompt, full history, and the filtered tool schema — never raw AgentScript syntax.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: A subagent system override replaces the global prompt entirely; the two are never merged.  \n* C: Full conversation history is included in every reasoning iteration, not withheld.  \n* D: The LLM never receives raw `.agent` file syntax, including `if`/`else` blocks."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "An architect is debugging a case where the agent produced an unexpected reply. Which diagnostic question does the two-phase split let them ask precisely first?", options: [("A", "Did the underlying foundation model experience a temporary outage during that specific reasoning iteration?"), ("B", "Did the wrong instructions reach the LLM in Phase 1, or did the LLM act incorrectly on the right instructions in Phase 2?"), ("C", "Did the customer's browser cache an outdated version of the conversation transcript before the message was sent?"), ("D", "Did the response exceed the maximum payload size, causing automatic truncation before delivery?")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: This is the core diagnostic framing the two-phase split enables — isolating whether the failure is a resolution problem or a reasoning problem.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Model outages are not the diagnostic concept this framing is built around.  \n* C: Browser-side caching is unrelated to the server-side two-phase reasoning split.  \n* D: Payload truncation is a distinct constraint, not what the Phase 1/Phase 2 split is designed to isolate."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "Which of the following correctly describes the three possible outcomes of a single LLM reasoning decision in Phase 2?", options: [("A", "Produce a terminal response, silently discard the user's message, or request additional LLM credits from the org admin."), ("B", "Call an action, modify an `available when` condition for a future turn, or restart the reasoning loop from turn one."), ("C", "Call an action, produce a terminal response, or delegate to a subagent available as a tool."), ("D", "Delegate to a subagent, rewrite the global system prompt for future turns, or terminate the entire session.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: These are the only three decisions available to the LLM at the end of a reasoning iteration.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Discarding messages or requesting credits are not decisions available to the LLM.  \n* B: The LLM cannot modify `available when` conditions or restart the loop from turn one; those aren't available decisions.  \n* D: Rewriting the global system prompt or terminating the session are not available LLM decisions."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A developer places a \"did the lookup succeed?\" check at the bottom of a `reasoning.instructions: ->` block, after the first-entry greeting text. What production symptom results?", options: [("A", "The check silently never executes, even once the loop exits, because bottom-placed checks are permanently unreachable code."), ("B", "The underlying action is automatically retried indefinitely by the FSM until the check's condition becomes true."), ("C", "The AgentScript compiler rejects the file outright, refusing to publish until the check is moved above the greeting text."), ("D", "The LLM sees the first-entry greeting text again on every re-resolution before ever reaching the check, causing repeated, confusing behavior.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Because the whole block re-resolves top-to-bottom after each tool call, a bottom-placed check is preceded by the same stale prompt text every single time.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The check does eventually run; the problem is what precedes it on each pass, not permanent unreachability.  \n* B: There's no indefinite-retry mechanism tied to check placement; this isn't how the FSM retry logic works.  \n* C: This is a silent logic bug, not something the compiler detects or rejects at publish time."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "Approximately how many reasoning iterations can occur within a single user turn before the runtime forces an exit?", options: [("A", "Roughly 3-4, as a guardrail against infinite reasoning loops."), ("B", "Exactly one, since each user turn is limited to a single LLM decision by design."), ("C", "Up to 20, matching the maximum number of actions permitted in a single subagent."), ("D", "Unlimited, since the loop only ends once a terminal response is produced, however long that takes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The reasoning loop is bounded to approximately 3-4 iterations per turn specifically to prevent runaway loops.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: A single iteration is too restrictive — multiple tool calls within one turn are explicitly supported before the cap is hit.  \n* C: 20 actions per subagent is not a described limit tied to reasoning iterations.  \n* D: The loop is explicitly bounded, not unlimited; this is exactly what the guardrail prevents."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "Which block executes exactly once per turn, strictly before the reasoning loop begins, and can prevent the LLM from ever being called that turn?", options: [("A", "`after_reasoning`, which always runs regardless of whether the loop produced a response."), ("B", "`before_reasoning`, which can fire a `transition to` and exit before the loop starts."), ("C", "`reasoning.instructions`, which re-resolves fresh on every single tool call within the loop."), ("D", "The Einstein Trust Layer's output screening pass, which inspects the LLM's response text.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: `before_reasoning` runs once before the loop and can transition away immediately, meaning the LLM is never invoked for that turn.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: `after_reasoning` runs after the loop produces a response, not before it begins.  \n* C: `reasoning.instructions` is part of the loop itself, not a pre-loop gate.  \n* D: Output screening happens after an LLM response is produced, the opposite point in the turn."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "What happens to a subagent's `after_reasoning` block if that subagent transitions away mid-reasoning via an LLM-driven handoff?", options: [("A", "It runs twice — once for the handoff and once for the destination subagent's own turn-end processing."), ("B", "It is queued and runs retroactively once the destination subagent eventually produces its own terminal response."), ("C", "It does not run at all, since `after_reasoning` only fires when the loop ends with a terminal response in that same subagent."), ("D", "It runs immediately at the moment the handoff fires, before the destination subagent's reasoning begins.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: `after_reasoning` is tied to a terminal response occurring within that subagent's own reasoning loop; a mid-reasoning handoff away from the subagent skips it entirely.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no double-firing behavior described for handoffs.  \n* B: There's no retroactive queuing mechanism for skipped `after_reasoning` blocks.  \n* D: `after_reasoning` is explicitly a post-loop hook; it does not fire \"immediately\" during a mid-loop handoff."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "A global `system.instructions` block says \"Answer the user's questions helpfully,\" while a router subagent's `reasoning.instructions` says \"Do not answer. Route only.\" What is the outcome?", options: [("A", "The subagent-level reasoning instructions always silently take priority over any conflicting global instruction, by design."), ("B", "The platform automatically detects the semantic contradiction and merges the two into a single coherent instruction."), ("C", "The global system instruction always wins because it is processed earlier in the resolution pipeline than reasoning instructions."), ("D", "Undefined behavior results, since both contradicting instructions reach the LLM with no guaranteed resolution.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Both instructions are delivered to the LLM simultaneously with no defined precedence rule, producing genuinely undefined behavior.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no rule making reasoning instructions automatically override a conflicting global instruction.  \n* B: No automatic contradiction-detection or merging mechanism exists in the reasoning pipeline.  \n* C: There's no processing-order precedence that makes the global instruction the guaranteed winner."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A subagent defines its own `system:` block with different content than the global one. What is the resulting behavior for that subagent?", options: [("A", "The subagent's override fully replaces the global instructions — any invariant not restated in the override no longer applies."), ("B", "The global instructions remain active underneath the override for safety-related content, while persona/tone content is replaced."), ("C", "The two blocks are appended together, so the LLM receives both the global and subagent-level instructions back-to-back."), ("D", "The subagent override is ignored entirely unless the global block explicitly grants permission for subagent-level overrides.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: A subagent system override is a total replacement — nothing from the global instructions carries over unless explicitly restated.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: There is no selective carry-over of \"safety-related\" content; replacement is total, not partial.  \n* C: Appending is not how overrides behave — the global block is fully dropped, not concatenated.  \n* D: No permission-granting mechanism in the global block is required for a subagent override to take effect."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "A developer writes a line beginning with `if @variables.is_verified == True:` inside a `reasoning.instructions: |` (pipe) block. What happens at runtime?", options: [("A", "The platform automatically converts the block to procedural mode during compilation, since conditional syntax was detected."), ("B", "The literal text of the `if` line is sent to the LLM as plain English prose, which it may or may not follow correctly."), ("C", "A compile-time error is raised immediately, since conditional keywords are forbidden inside literal-mode blocks."), ("D", "The condition is evaluated deterministically exactly as it would be in procedural mode, since `if` always triggers resolution.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Literal mode performs no evaluation at all — the raw text, including the `if` keyword, is passed to the LLM verbatim as prose.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no automatic mode conversion; this ships exactly as authored, silently.  \n* C: No compile-time error occurs — this is precisely why the mistake is easy to miss.  \n* D: Deterministic evaluation happens only in procedural mode, never in literal mode, regardless of keywords present."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "Which statement correctly describes `before_reasoning` and `after_reasoning` with respect to the `instructions:` wrapper?", options: [("A", "Both blocks require an `instructions: ->` wrapper, identical in syntax to `reasoning.instructions`."), ("B", "`before_reasoning` requires literal mode while `after_reasoning` requires procedural mode, matching their different timing."), ("C", "Neither block supports an `instructions:` wrapper at all — content is written directly, and wrapping it is a compile error."), ("D", "Both blocks are handed directly to the LLM as additional system-level context alongside the global system prompt.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Both lifecycle hooks take direct, unwrapped content; adding an `instructions:` wrapper to either one is a documented compile error.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Neither block uses the `instructions:` wrapper syntax at all.  \n* B: There is no mode distinction between the two blocks, since neither supports wrapped instruction modes.  \n* D: Both blocks are processed entirely by the deterministic resolver; the LLM never sees their content directly."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "A developer declares `is_verified: mutable boolean = false` using a lowercase literal. What happens at compile time?", options: [("A", "The variable silently defaults to `True` instead of `False`, since lowercase literals are treated as inverted values."), ("B", "The variable becomes permanently read-only for the session, since lowercase declarations are treated as constants."), ("C", "The declaration compiles successfully, since AgentScript treats boolean literals as fully case-insensitive."), ("D", "A compile error occurs, since the parser requires capitalized `True`/`False` and rejects lowercase boolean literals.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Boolean literals must be capitalized; lowercase is an explicit, common compile error, not a silently accepted variant.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There's no described value-inversion behavior tied to lowercase literals — it's a hard error, not a semantic quirk.  \n* B: Lowercase casing has no effect on mutability; that's governed by the `mutable`/`linked` keyword, not letter case.  \n* C: Boolean case sensitivity is explicit; there is no case-insensitive fallback."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "Immediately after a `run @actions.X` block completes, a developer writes `set @variables.station = @inputs.station_name` using the same input that was passed via `with station_name = ...`. What happens?", options: [("A", "The assignment fails silently — `@inputs` values are valid only inside the `with` clause during invocation, not afterward."), ("B", "The assignment throws a visible runtime error, halting the turn and surfacing a clear scope-violation message."), ("C", "The assignment succeeds normally, since `@inputs` values remain valid for the remainder of the current turn."), ("D", "The assignment succeeds only if the action's output schema explicitly re-exports the input value under the same name.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: `@inputs` scope is limited strictly to the invocation's `with` clause; referencing it afterward is a documented silent failure with no visible error.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: No visible runtime error is thrown — this is exactly what makes the failure dangerous.  \n* C: `@inputs` scope expires immediately after invocation; it does not persist for the rest of the turn.  \n* D: There's no re-export mechanism described that would make this assignment succeed."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "Which variable type must declare a `source` attribute and must NOT declare a default value?", options: [("A", "Mutable variables, which always default to reading from session context rather than a literal value."), ("B", "Linked variables, which are read-only and populated from external session context such as a messaging session."), ("C", "`@outputs` values, since they are always derived from an action's return payload rather than a static default."), ("D", "`@inputs` values, since the LLM populates them dynamically during the slot-filling process.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Linked variables are read-only, require a `source`, and explicitly cannot declare a default — this combination is unique to them.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Mutable variables require a default value; they have no `source` concept at all.  \n* C: `@outputs` values aren't declared with `source`/default syntax — they're transient, action-scoped values.  \n* D: `@inputs` values are similarly transient and scoped to the invocation's `with` clause, not declared with a `source`."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "A workflow verifies a customer's identity in one subagent and then transitions to a second subagent that must respect that verification. What is the correct mechanism for this?", options: [("A", "Re-run identity verification from scratch in the second subagent, since state never survives a subagent transition."), ("B", "Store the verification result only in `@outputs.is_verified`, since output scope extends indefinitely across transitions."), ("C", "Rely on variable persistence — a mutable variable set in the first subagent remains available in the second after transition."), ("D", "Store the result only in `@inputs.is_verified`, since input scope is the sole mechanism that survives a transition.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Variables persist across subagent transitions, which is exactly the mechanism that lets a downstream subagent trust an upstream verification result.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This contradicts variable persistence, which explicitly holds state across transitions.  \n* B: `@outputs` scope expires immediately after the capturing statements, long before any transition occurs.  \n* D: `@inputs` scope is even more limited, valid only during the originating action's own invocation."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "What distinct side effect does `@utils.setVariables` have that a normal LLM slot-fill directly on an action does not?", options: [("A", "It permanently converts the captured variables into read-only linked variables for the rest of the session."), ("B", "It triggers the `after_reasoning` block to execute twice — once for the capture and once for anything that follows."), ("C", "It bypasses the Einstein Trust Layer entirely, since slot-filling utilities are exempt from runtime screening."), ("D", "It ends the turn immediately after capturing values, so any subsequent action in the same block will not fire that turn.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: `@utils.setVariables` ends the current turn once it captures values; anything chained after it in the same block waits until the next turn to fire.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Captured variables remain fully mutable; they are not converted into read-only linked variables.  \n* B: There is no double-firing of `after_reasoning` tied to this utility's turn-ending behavior.  \n* C: The Trust Layer wraps every LLM call; there's no exemption carved out for this utility."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "An architect needs an action to run before the LLM ever reasons, regardless of what the LLM might otherwise decide. Which invocation mode guarantees this?", options: [("A", "Deterministic invocation via `run @actions.X`, which always executes when its code path is reached, independent of LLM choice."), ("B", "LLM-driven invocation via the `reasoning.actions` block, since the LLM calls every listed action once per turn automatically."), ("C", "Wrapping the action with `available when True`, which forces immediate invocation on the very first reasoning iteration."), ("D", "Setting the action's `is_used_by_planner` flag to true, which schedules automatic pre-LLM execution.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: `run @actions.X` fires deterministically in Phase 1, before the LLM is ever invoked, regardless of what the LLM would otherwise choose.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: LLM-driven actions fire only when the LLM decides to call them — never automatically on every turn.  \n* C: `available when` only controls tool schema visibility to the LLM; it does not force invocation.  \n* D: `is_used_by_planner` controls whether the LLM can reason about an output value, not whether an action executes automatically."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A refund action must never be callable unless the customer's identity is verified. Which control provides a hard guarantee rather than a probabilistic hint?", options: [("A", "A prose instruction in `reasoning.instructions` telling the LLM to only call the action if verified, trusting it to comply."), ("B", "`available when @variables.is_verified == True`, which removes the action from the LLM's tool schema entirely when false."), ("C", "Setting the action's description to mention \"verified customers only,\" relying on the LLM to infer the constraint."), ("D", "A comment in the `.agent` file noting that verification should be checked before this action is invoked.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: `available when` is a hard, compiler-enforced filter — when false, the action is completely absent from what the LLM can see or call, unlike prose or description hints.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Prose instructions are probabilistic; the LLM may or may not comply, which is unacceptable for an irreversible action.  \n* C: Description text is also just an interpretive hint, not an enforced constraint.  \n* D: A comment has zero runtime effect and enforces nothing."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "Which two statements correctly describe the difference between a one-way handoff and two-way delegation between subagents? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "In delegation, the child subagent runs its own reasoning loop and returns control to the caller, which synthesizes the final response."), ("B", "In a handoff, control transfers completely to the destination subagent and never returns to the caller."), ("C", "Delegation is only available in `before_reasoning`, while handoffs are only available in `after_reasoning`."), ("D", "A handoff always requires the destination subagent to have its own `system:` override, while delegation does not.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Delegation is a two-way call where control returns to the caller; a handoff is a one-way transfer where the caller never resumes control.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Both mechanisms are LLM-driven decisions available within `reasoning.actions`, not tied to the lifecycle hooks.  \n* D: Neither mechanism has a requirement tied to whether the destination subagent defines its own system override."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "What is the purpose of combining `filter_from_agent: True` with `is_used_by_planner: True` on an action's output?", options: [("A", "It automatically retries the action up to three times if the LLM attempts to reference the value before it has been set."), ("B", "It causes the output value to be logged permanently for compliance purposes while hiding it from the conversation transcript."), ("C", "It forces the LLM to actually invoke the action to obtain the value — preventing hallucination — while letting it route on that value without ever displaying it."), ("D", "It converts the output into a linked variable so it persists automatically across every future subagent transition.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: This flag combination is exactly what prevents the LLM from guessing at a value while still allowing it to use that value for internal routing decisions.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: No automatic retry mechanism is tied to referencing an unset output value.  \n* B: There's no permanent compliance-logging behavior tied to this flag combination.  \n* D: This combination does not convert an output into a linked variable; it only controls visibility and planner usability."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "A regulated workflow must have zero LLM discretion over sequencing or routing decisions. Which posture should be used?", options: [("A", "Agentic posture, since maximum LLM flexibility is always preferable when regulatory compliance is at stake."), ("B", "Whichever posture the underlying foundation model is best tuned for, since posture is a model property rather than an authoring choice."), ("C", "Mixed posture, since combining some LLM discretion with partial gates is inherently stricter than full determinism."), ("D", "Scripted posture, where all transitions use bare `transition to` and all actions use `run`, removing LLM discretion entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Scripted posture removes all LLM discretion by using only bare transitions and deterministic `run` calls, matching a zero-variance regulatory requirement exactly.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Agentic posture maximizes flexibility, the opposite of what a zero-discretion requirement needs.  \n* B: Posture is an authoring choice expressed in AgentScript, not an inherent property of the underlying model.  \n* C: Mixed posture still grants some discretion to the LLM, which fails a genuinely zero-discretion requirement."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "Which of the following is NOT one of the accepted justifications for adding deterministic control to a subagent?", options: [("A", "The architect simply finds deterministic code more elegant to read during a client review."), ("B", "The action being controlled is irreversible, such as a refund or a delete."), ("C", "A regulatory requirement mandates a specific sequence or disclosure."), ("D", "A specific behavior has already failed in production and determinism is the fix.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Aesthetic or stylistic preference is explicitly not a valid justification — determinism should be added only for concrete, named reasons.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Irreversibility of the action is an accepted justification for adding deterministic control.  \n* C: Regulatory requirement is an accepted justification for adding deterministic control.  \n* D: An observed production failure is an accepted justification for adding deterministic control."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "Which three primitives are used to implement mixed posture in a subagent? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "`available when` guards that hard-remove an action from the tool schema based on a condition."), ("B", "Parameter pinning — binding a parameter to `@variables.X` instead of `...` to remove LLM slot-fill discretion."), ("C", "Conditional instructions using `if`/`else` in procedural mode, so the LLM only sees the matching branch."), ("D", "Dynamically adjusting the underlying foundation model's temperature setting per subagent to reduce variance.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 2], explanation: "Why correct: These three primitives — hard action gating, parameter pinning, and conditional instruction branching — are exactly how mixed posture blends determinism with LLM latitude.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: There is no described per-subagent temperature-adjustment primitive used to implement mixed posture."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "Why does minimal instruction authoring generally produce better reasoning outcomes?", options: [("A", "Shorter instructions are billed at a lower credit rate per character, directly reducing operating cost."), ("B", "Concise, focused instructions reduce conflicting signals for the model to interpret and help stay within the effective token budget."), ("C", "The AgentScript compiler enforces a strict character limit on every `reasoning.instructions` block, making brevity mandatory."), ("D", "Long instructions cause the LLM to ignore all tool schemas entirely, regardless of their content.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Minimal instructions reduce interpretive conflict for the model and help the reasoning payload stay within its effective performance budget — improving latency and reasoning accuracy.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Credit billing is tied to action executions, not the character count of instruction text.  \n* C: There is no hard character-limit enforced by the compiler on instruction blocks.  \n* D: Long instructions do not cause total disregard of tool schemas; the effect described is a gradual quality/latency degradation, not an on/off failure."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "A client asks whether their customer data used for grounding will be used to train the underlying foundation model's future public releases. What is the accurate answer?", options: [("A", "Yes, but only for the specific customer's own account, and only if that customer opts out of a separate governance setting."), ("B", "Yes, all grounding data is retained by the model provider by default unless a specific retention-opt-out is manually configured."), ("C", "No, because the Trust Layer's zero-retention policy discards grounding data immediately after the specific inference call."), ("D", "It depends entirely on which foundation model is configured, since only some providers offer any zero-retention guarantee at all.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Zero-retention is a contractual and architectural guarantee — grounding data is discarded immediately after the inference call and never used for external model training.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This describes an opt-out mechanism that does not exist; zero-retention applies by default, not conditionally.  \n* B: This reverses the actual guarantee — retention is not the default behavior at all.  \n* D: The zero-retention guarantee is a Trust Layer architectural property, not something that varies by which model is configured."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "Where does the Einstein Trust Layer sit within a single reasoning iteration?", options: [("A", "Entirely after the LLM produces its terminal response, screening only what the user ultimately sees."), ("B", "Only during agent publish, as a one-time static analysis pass over the compiled Agent Graph."), ("C", "Only inside `before_reasoning`, since that is the sole point where deterministic security screening is possible."), ("D", "Between Phase 1's resolved output and the LLM call, and again on the way back before the response re-enters the reasoning loop.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The Trust Layer wraps the LLM call on both sides — screening the assembled context going in and the model's output coming back — for every single reasoning iteration.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Screening also happens on the way in, before the LLM call, not only after the response is produced.  \n* B: This describes a one-time authoring-time analysis, which is a distinct concept from the per-call runtime Trust Layer.  \n* C: The Trust Layer operates around every LLM call generally, not confined to the `before_reasoning` hook."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "How does the Einstein Trust Layer enforce data boundaries for grounding data pulled into an LLM call?", options: [("A", "It applies the same sharing rules and field-level security the running user would face in a direct query, extended to the LLM boundary."), ("B", "It only enforces boundaries for actions invoked deterministically via `run`, leaving LLM-driven actions ungoverned."), ("C", "It grants the LLM broader access than the running user has, on the assumption that the LLM itself is a trusted internal system."), ("D", "It removes all field-level security checks for grounding data specifically, since grounding is treated as a system-level operation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The Trust Layer extends the running user's existing sharing rules and field-level security to the LLM boundary, so grounding data respects the same permission model as any direct query.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Data boundary enforcement applies to actions regardless of invocation mode, not selectively to deterministic ones.  \n* C: The LLM is not granted broader access than the running user; the same restrictions apply.  \n* D: Field-level security is not waived for grounding; the same checks apply as for any other data access."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "A workflow's execution path could require 6 sequential LLM-driven action calls within a single user turn. What is the architectural implication?", options: [("A", "This is fully supported without changes, since the reasoning loop has no meaningful cap on iterations per turn."), ("B", "The workflow must be redesigned to spread the decisions across multiple user turns, since the loop caps out at roughly 3-4 iterations."), ("C", "The workflow will succeed, but each additional call beyond the fourth will be billed at double the standard credit rate."), ("D", "The sixth call will automatically be converted into a deterministic `run` call to bypass the iteration cap.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: With a roughly 3-4 iteration cap per turn, any path requiring more sequential LLM-driven decisions must be broken up across multiple turns.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The loop is explicitly bounded; treating it as uncapped will produce a forced exit partway through.  \n* C: There is no double-billing mechanism tied to exceeding the iteration cap.  \n* D: There's no automatic conversion of LLM-driven calls into deterministic ones when the cap is approached."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "What is the primary consequence of a reasoning payload growing well beyond the effective ~10,000 token heuristic?", options: [("A", "The agent's monthly credit bill increases proportionally to the number of tokens processed in that turn."), ("B", "The action's underlying Apex class throws a governor limit exception once the payload crosses the threshold."), ("C", "Response quality degrades and latency spikes become measurable, since every extra token adds interpretive load for the model."), ("D", "The org's maximum number of active or committed agents is reduced by one for each oversized payload.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The 10k heuristic is a performance and accuracy target — exceeding it measurably degrades response quality and increases latency, not cost.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Credit billing is tied to action executions, not token counts, so this has no bearing on the monthly bill.  \n* B: Apex governor limits are unrelated to LLM context payload size; this isn't the described consequence.  \n* D: There's no mechanism reducing an org's agent limit based on payload size in any given turn."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "Which two of the following are effective levers for keeping a reasoning payload within the token budget? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Aggressively using `available when` to suppress tool schemas for actions that are irrelevant in the current state."), ("B", "Consolidating redundant actions so the tool schema doesn't carry near-duplicate options for the same task."), ("C", "Increasing the reasoning loop's iteration cap from 4 to 10 so fewer tokens are needed per iteration."), ("D", "Reducing the number of active or committed agents in the org to free up shared token capacity.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Suppressing irrelevant tool schemas and consolidating redundant actions both directly reduce what gets sent to the LLM each iteration.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: The iteration cap is a per-turn loop guardrail unrelated to per-iteration token payload size, and it isn't user-adjustable in this way.  \n* D: Token budget is per-payload, per-turn; the org-wide agent count has no bearing on an individual reasoning iteration's token size."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "An architect reviews a `reasoning.instructions: |` block that contains `if @variables.is_verified == True:` followed by response text. What is the fix?", options: [("A", "Delete the `if` line entirely, since literal mode cannot express any conditional behavior under any circumstance."), ("B", "Leave it as-is, since the LLM reliably interprets `if` keywords in literal mode with the same accuracy as procedural mode."), ("C", "Move the `if` line into `before_reasoning`, since only lifecycle hooks can evaluate conditions in AgentScript."), ("D", "Switch the block to procedural mode (`->`) so the condition is actually evaluated before the LLM ever sees the response text.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Switching to procedural mode is the only way to make the condition actually evaluate deterministically rather than being passed to the LLM as unevaluated prose.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Deleting the condition removes the intended logic rather than fixing how it's expressed.  \n* B: The LLM treats the `if` keyword as literal English prose in this mode and may not follow it reliably at all.  \n* C: `reasoning.instructions` in procedural mode can evaluate conditions directly; moving to a lifecycle hook is unnecessary and changes the trigger timing."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A developer writes `@utils.setVariables` to capture an order ID, immediately followed by an action call using that same order ID, both inside the same `reasoning.actions` block, expecting both to fire in one turn. What is wrong?", options: [("A", "`setVariables` ends the turn once it captures the value, so the following action will not fire until the next turn."), ("B", "`setVariables` is a Phase 1 deterministic operation and cannot legally appear inside a `reasoning.actions` block at all."), ("C", "The order ID must be renamed to avoid colliding with a reserved system keyword, or the block will fail to compile."), ("D", "Nothing is wrong — both will fire correctly in the declared sequence within the same turn, since actions execute in order.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: `setVariables` ends the current turn immediately after capturing values, meaning any subsequent action in the same block is deferred to the next turn rather than firing immediately.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: `setVariables` is a valid entry inside `reasoning.actions`; it does not cause a compile error there.  \n* C: There's no reserved-keyword collision issue at play in this specific scenario.  \n* D: This is precisely the incorrect assumption the pattern warns against — the second action does not fire in the same turn."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "An architect finds an action description that simply reads \"Get data\" attached to a customer-lookup action. What risk does this create?", options: [("A", "The action will be permanently hidden from the LLM's tool schema until the description is lengthened past a minimum threshold."), ("B", "The LLM cannot reliably distinguish this action from other similarly generic actions, risking incorrect tool selection."), ("C", "The Einstein Trust Layer will block the action from executing due to insufficient descriptive metadata."), ("D", "The action will execute automatically in `before_reasoning` regardless of the LLM's own decision-making.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A vague description gives the LLM insufficient signal to distinguish this action from others with similarly generic purposes, increasing the risk of incorrect tool selection during Phase 2.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no minimum-length threshold that hides an action from the tool schema based on description length.  \n* C: The Trust Layer screens for security/toxicity concerns, not descriptive metadata quality.  \n* D: Description quality has no bearing on whether an action fires deterministically in `before_reasoning`."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "A subagent's own `system:` override says only \"You are a technical specialist. Be detailed,\" dropping a global invariant that forbade revealing internal configuration details. What is the risk, and the fix?", options: [("A", "There is no risk, since safety invariants automatically persist beneath any subagent-level override regardless of its content."), ("B", "The risk only applies in voice-modality deployments; text-channel subagents inherit all global invariants regardless of overrides."), ("C", "The subagent may now reveal internal configuration since the invariant was never restated; the fix is to explicitly restate every required invariant in the override."), ("D", "The fix is to remove the subagent's override entirely, since subagent-level system overrides are never permitted alongside a global system block.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A subagent-level system override completely replaces the global instructions, so any invariant not explicitly restated in the override no longer applies to that subagent — the fix is restating it directly.  \nWhy each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Safety invariants are not automatically preserved beneath an override; replacement is total, not selective.  \n* B: This risk applies regardless of modality; nothing in the mechanism is voice-specific.  \n* D: Subagent-level overrides are a supported, valid construct; removing the override entirely is a more drastic fix than necessary.\n\n---"),
        Question(id: "40", question: "A developer is configuring a secure refund subagent. The refund action requires the customer's verified account ID, which was confirmed during an earlier identity-verification subagent. The developer wants to guarantee the LLM cannot substitute a different account ID. Which approach provides the strongest security?", options: [("A", "Add a prose instruction telling the LLM: \"Always use the verified account ID from the identity check.\""), ("B", "Bind the parameter to @variables.verified_account_id using session-persistent variable binding instead of the LLM slot-fill operator (...)."), ("C", "Set the action's description to mention that only verified IDs are accepted."), ("D", "Use available when @variables.is_verified == True on the refund action and let the LLM fill the account ID freely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Parameter pinning — binding directly to @variables.verified_account_id instead of using the LLM slot-fill operator (...) — removes LLM discretion entirely, guaranteeing the exact verified value is used every time. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Prose instructions are probabilistic hints the LLM may not follow, especially for security-critical parameters.\n* C: Description text is interpretive guidance, not an enforced constraint — the LLM can still substitute any value.\n* D: available when controls whether the action appears in the tool schema but does not constrain which parameter value the LLM chooses once the action is visible."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "A developer suspects that an @outputs value captured from an action is not available when a subsequent action tries to use it two reasoning iterations later. Which diagnostic step most directly confirms the scope expiration?", options: [("A", "Check the action's Apex class to see if it returns the value in its output schema."), ("B", "Add a prose instruction reminding the LLM to remember the value across iterations."), ("C", "Inspect the trace visualization to confirm whether the FunctionStep's postVars still contain the @outputs value at the point of the second action's invocation."), ("D", "Redeploy the agent with verbose logging enabled on the Einstein Trust Layer.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The trace visualization's FunctionStep postVars show exactly which variables and outputs are in scope at each point in the reasoning chain — this directly reveals whether an @outputs value has expired by the time the second action fires. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The Apex class returning the value correctly does not tell you whether the runtime still holds it in scope two iterations later.\n* B: Prose instructions cannot extend @outputs scope — scope expiration is a runtime mechanism, not an LLM compliance issue.\n* D: Trust Layer logging covers input/output screening, not variable scope lifecycle."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "An architect wants a subagent to route the user to a specialized handler immediately — before the LLM reasons at all — when a specific variable is already set. Which mechanism achieves pre-LLM routing?", options: [("A", "A bare transition to in before_reasoning with a conditional guard on the variable."), ("B", "An available when guard on an action in reasoning.actions that transitions when called."), ("C", "A prose instruction in reasoning.instructions telling the LLM to transition immediately if the variable is set."), ("D", "Setting the variable as a linked variable with source pointing to the destination subagent.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: before_reasoning executes exactly once per turn, strictly before the reasoning loop begins. A bare transition to inside it — guarded by a condition on the variable — routes the user immediately without the LLM ever being invoked. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: available when only controls tool schema visibility during Phase 2; it does not execute before the LLM reasons.\n* C: Prose instructions are processed by the LLM during Phase 2, which means the LLM has already been invoked — this is not pre-LLM routing.\n* D: Linked variables populate from external session context (like messaging sessions); they do not trigger transitions or routing."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "A developer wants to inspect the compiled structure of their agent to verify that the DAG and FSM were built correctly before publishing to production. Which artifact should they examine?", options: [("A", "The raw .agent file opened in a text editor, since the DAG/FSM structure is visible in the syntax."), ("B", "The Einstein Trust Layer's audit log, which records the compiled graph structure."), ("C", "The Apex test results from the action classes, which include graph validation assertions."), ("D", "The GenAiPlannerBundle metadata, which contains the compiled Agent Graph with the DAG for tool dependencies and FSM for subagent transitions.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The GenAiPlannerBundle is the compiled output that contains the Agent Graph — including the DAG encoding tool dependency ordering and the FSM encoding subagent transitions and retry edges. Inspecting this artifact directly reveals the compiled structure. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The raw .agent file is the source, not the compiled output — you cannot see the DAG/FSM structure in the uncompiled syntax.\n* B: The Trust Layer's audit log covers per-call input/output screening, not the compiled graph structure.\n* C: Apex test results validate action logic, not the agent's compiled transition and dependency graph."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "An architect is optimizing a subagent that currently exposes 12 actions to the LLM, several of which are only relevant in specific workflow states. The reasoning payload is exceeding the ~10,000 token heuristic. Which two changes most effectively reduce cognitive load on the LLM and billing costs? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Add available when guards to suppress irrelevant actions based on current workflow state variables."), ("B", "Increase the reasoning loop iteration cap to allow more iterations with smaller payloads."), ("C", "Consolidate redundant actions that perform similar tasks into fewer, well-described actions."), ("D", "Move all 12 actions into before_reasoning as deterministic run calls.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: available when guards remove irrelevant actions from the tool schema entirely when their conditions are false, directly shrinking the per-iteration payload. Consolidating redundant actions reduces duplicate schema entries the LLM must parse. Both directly address cognitive load and token budget. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: The iteration cap is a per-turn guardrail (~3-4 iterations) and is not user-adjustable; it also does not reduce per-iteration payload size.\n* D: Moving all actions to deterministic run removes LLM discretion entirely, which changes the agent's behavior fundamentally rather than optimizing the existing agentic posture."),
    ]
}
